// A `let` mismatch between two instantiations of the same generic ENUM printed both sides with their type arguments
// dropped, so the sentence claimed the two types were the same:
//   error [fn main]: let 'b': type mismatch — expected Opt, got Opt
// Right verdict, useless sentence. Control revert on the pre-fix binary 5c2889a91: that exact text; on this binary:
//   error [fn main]: let 'b': type mismatch — expected Opt<i32>, got Opt<i64>
//
// WHAT CLOSED IT. `expect_type` renders through `type_str_pair`, whose `need_qual` only qualifies same-named types
// from DIFFERENT packages; two `Opt`s from one package differing only in their argument fell through to plain
// `type_str`, and `type_str`'s Enum arm returns the bare `enum_name()` unless `source_form` is set — while its Struct
// arm always carries the arguments, which is why the row's own `W<i32>` / `W<i64>` control printed correctly. The fix
// keys on the SYMPTOM, not on a carrier list: `expect_type` reaches its error only on a mismatch, so two renderings
// that COLLIDE mean the printer lost what distinguishes them, and only then is the pair re-rendered with `source_form`.
// ⚠ `type_str`'s Enum arm is also reached from `mangle_type_for_name`, which composes SYMBOL names — widening it there
// would have changed the ABI. The re-render lives in the diagnostic path alone.
//
// The original row header follows.
// SOUNDNESS QUEUE row generic_enum_type_printed_without_args (tier 4).
// Right verdict, useless sentence. A `let` type mismatch between two
// instantiations of the same generic ENUM prints both sides with their type
// ARGUMENTS DROPPED, so the message says the two types are the same:
//   error [fn main]: let 'b': type mismatch — expected Opt, got Opt
//
// ⚠ MEASURED 2026-09-08 on build 2c5a33ef99e94fc9 43, with the control that
// separates the printer from the checker: the SAME `let` mismatch over a
// generic STRUCT prints the arguments correctly —
//   struct W<T> { v: T }   let a: W<i64> = ...; let b: W<i32> = a;
//   error [fn main]: let 'b': type mismatch — expected W<i32>, got W<i64>
// so it is the generic-ENUM spelling of the type printer, not the `let` site
// and not generics as such. The defect is reachable with no coercion anywhere
// in the program (this one), which is what separates it from the row
// enum_variant_ctor_arg_no_unsize_coercion, where the same nonsense sentence
// also appears (`Opt::S(&a)` at `Opt<&[i64]>`). ⚠ That sibling row no longer
// exists — neither its program nor a ledger entry — so the cross-reference is
// stale; it was retired before this round.
//
// Provenance: found 2026-09-08 varying the shape of the enum-ctor coercion row.
package generic_enum_type_printed_with_args;
enum Opt<T> { N, S(T) }
fn main() -> i32 {
    let a: Opt<i64> = Opt::S(1i64);
    let b: Opt<i32> = a;
    return 0i32;
}
